<Base title="ASCII FX — matcher internals" active="internals">
  <h1>Matcher internals</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The normative definition lives in <code>ALGORITHM.md</code> — every constant, bit layout, and tie-break.
    This page is the guided tour.
  </p>

  <pre class="diagram">
                         BUILD TIME
                             │
              font ──────────┤ deterministic rasterizer
                             ▼
                     ┌──────────────┐
                     │ AsciiProfile │  masks · coverage · atlas
                     └──────┬───────┘
SOURCE  image/video/canvas/three
        │                   │
        ▼                   ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────┐
│          EXACT MATCHER            │
│  box-filter to 8×8 samples/cell   │
│  ↓ binary structural mask         │
│  ↓ Hamming top-8 prefilter        │
│  ↓ integer RGB rerank + fit       │
└─────────────────┬─────────────────┘
                  ▼
             ASCII FIELD  (glyph ids + fitted colors)
                  ▼
        one-draw atlas composite  ←  interactions live here
  </pre>

  <h2>1 · Reduction</h2>
  <p>
    The source is area-averaged with an integer box filter to exactly <code>8·columns × 8·rows</code> samples.
    Hardware texture filtering is never used for matching — it isn't bit-exact across GPUs. Alpha-weighted
    sampling keeps transparent garbage out of the structure.
  </p>

  <h2>2 · Cell features and the mask</h2>
  <p>
    Per cell: integer luminance <code>(77R + 150G + 29B + 128) &gt;&gt; 8</code>, min/max luma with
    lowest-index tie-breaks, and means. Cells whose luma spread is under 15 take the flat path (coverage-mapped
    glyph). Otherwise every sample classifies to the nearer of the darkest/lightest sample by squared RGB
    distance → a 64-bit structural mask.
  </p>

  <h2>3 · Prefilter</h2>
  <p>
    Popcount XOR against every glyph mask; keep the 8 smallest <code>(distance, glyphId)</code> keys. In full
    color the score is <code>min(d, 64−d)</code> — a glyph and its complement reconstruct identically once both
    colors are fitted, so orientation is absorbed by color assignment rather than spent as candidate slots.
  </p>

  <h2>4 · Exact rerank</h2>
  <p>
    Each candidate partitions the 64 samples by its own ink mask; foreground/background means are fitted
    (u8-rounded — part of the algorithm), and the winner minimizes total squared RGB reconstruction error.
    Everything is integer arithmetic: max error is 64·3·255² ≈ 12.5M, comfortably u32. Integer addition is
    associative, so GPU reduction order cannot change a result — that is what makes "100% CPU↔GPU agreement"
    a testable claim instead of a caveat.
  </p>

  <h2>Design decisions worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>No invert flag.</b> Polarity derives from the palette (<code>luma(fg) ≥ luma(bg)</code>): the rerank against fixed colors decides polarity anyway, so a free flag could only make the shortlist fight the objective. Swap the colors and everything flips coherently.</li>
    <li><b>Thin glyphs have empty masks.</b> <code>_ - .</code> legitimately binarize to nothing at the ≥0.5 area threshold — mirroring how a thin source line also blends toward the light endpoint. They're selected by the flat path via coverage. Symmetric, intended.</li>
    <li><b>Tonal range is charset-bound in mono.</b> ASCII text glyphs top out around 18% coverage, and both the flat path and the rerank saturate there identically (no seams). Wide-range sources want <code>ascii-blocks</code> or full color.</li>
    <li><b>Interactions never rematch.</b> Pointer, time, reveal radius, distortion — all composite-stage uniforms. The matcher re-dispatches only when the source or matcher inputs change (dirty-flag taxonomy, spec §20).</li>
    <li><b>Temporal reuse is exact.</b> With <code>temporal: true</code>, a cell skips matching only when all 64 sample bytes are identical to the previous frame — semantics-preserving by construction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Formats</h2>
  <p>
    <code>.asciip</code> (profile): versioned little-endian sections — glyph table, 64-bit masks, coverage,
    R8 atlas (power-of-two glyph pitch so padding survives mip levels), optional shape6 data, metadata; content
    fingerprints embedded; byte-for-byte reproducible builds. <code>.asciif</code> (frame): matched glyph ids +
    channel-planar colors referencing the profile by fingerprint — a 160-column full-color frame is ~22KB brotli.
  </p>

  <h2>Approximate matchers</h2>
  <p>
    <code>shape6</code> reduces each cell to six directional-contrast integers and nearest-neighbors against
    per-glyph vectors (optionally through a 262,144-entry compile-time LUT). <code>ramp</code> is coverage-by-
    brightness. Both are explicit opt-ins with published recall/error/speedup numbers — never a silent fallback.
  </p>
</Base>
